WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.134
Hi, I'm Kumquat!

00:00:02.134 --> 00:00:05.231
A decision model like Jev never writes text: it picks from

00:00:05.231 --> 00:00:06.357
a list you send.

00:00:06.357 --> 00:00:08.327
Here's how, and where to use one.

00:00:10.475 --> 00:00:13.883
The key fact: each option is scored on its own, and the

00:00:13.883 --> 00:00:16.438
options meet only in a softmax at the end.

00:00:16.438 --> 00:00:18.142
So no option can read another.

00:00:21.342 --> 00:00:23.003
Your code builds the option list.

00:00:23.003 --> 00:00:25.771
The model can only answer with something already on it.

00:00:26.191 --> 00:00:29.511
Each option is encoded in its own row, beside the state and

00:00:29.511 --> 00:00:31.724
the question, and comes out as one number.

00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:35.662
The options meet for the first time in a softmax, after every

00:00:35.662 --> 00:00:36.834
forward pass has finished.

00:00:37.254 --> 00:00:41.466
Then code does the rest: take the top option, or branch on its probability.

00:00:43.192 --> 00:00:46.107
One: is the answer set finite, and known before the call?

00:00:46.527 --> 00:00:48.355
Two: can code you own enumerate it?

00:00:48.355 --> 00:00:49.661
That's where the work is.

00:00:50.081 --> 00:00:52.900
Three: can each option be judged without reading another?

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.658
If not, build the relation in code, and ask about one pair.

00:00:57.078 --> 00:00:59.619
Four: is the list frozen before you branch?

00:00:59.619 --> 00:01:03.431
Add paraphrases of the right answer, and its probability falls from point

00:01:03.431 --> 00:01:05.973
six one nine to point two one five.

00:01:07.092 --> 00:01:11.024
On questions where one option refers to another, Jev scores zero

00:01:11.024 --> 00:01:12.454
out of a hundred.

00:01:12.454 --> 00:01:16.744
Letting options see each other isn't enough: a bidirectional encoder scores eight.

00:01:18.475 --> 00:01:21.471
So the test is whether one option can read another.

00:01:21.471 --> 00:01:22.969
Cost and latency aren't gates.

00:01:22.969 --> 00:01:25.366
This is the one money can't buy back.

00:01:27.192 --> 00:01:31.369
To recap: code builds the list, each option is scored alone, and

00:01:31.369 --> 00:01:33.457
relations between options belong in code.

00:01:33.877 --> 00:01:36.022
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:36.022 --> 00:01:36.634
I'm Kumquat.

00:01:36.634 --> 00:01:36.941
Bye!
